# Python-generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info

# Virtual environments
.venv

# Test scratch space (project-local pytest basetemp)
.pytest-tmp/

# pytest's own cache. It writes a .gitignore of its own inside the directory, but that is pytest's
# behaviour to change, not ours — ignore it here explicitly instead of relying on it.
.pytest_cache/

# Secrets: never commit these, in any form. The last matching pattern is the one git reports, so
# `.secrets/` is listed after the basename rules and is the rule `git check-ignore` names for a file
# inside it. Nothing in this section is a substitute for the others; each covers a different way a
# credential file gets written.
.env
*.env
.env.*
*.secret
*.secrets
.secrets/

# The example directory is a committed template, so its file is re-included explicitly. Without
# this, the `*.env` rule above would ignore it — and that file is the one meant to be committed.
# (A negation cannot resurrect a file whose parent directory is ignored, which is why `.secrets/`
# above is a directory rule and this one names the directory separately.)
!.secrets.example/
!.secrets.example/typesafe.env

# Run artifacts. Two files in `results/` are part of the frozen evaluation and are tracked: the
# canonical log, and the report derived from it. Everything else here is a regenerable view.
#
# The rule is `results/*` and then a negation per tracked file, rather than `results/*` blanket:
# a tracked canonical log is what makes an evaluation citable, and a clone without it has the
# bench but not the measurements. The derived files stay ignored on purpose -- they are rebuilt
# from the log by `report`, `snapshot`, and `final-report`, and a checked-in copy of a derived
# file is a second source of truth that can drift.
#
# A negation cannot resurrect a file whose *parent directory* is ignored, which is why the pattern
# is `results/*` and not `results/` -- the directory itself is not ignored, so these work.
results/*
!results/usage.jsonl
!results/JEV_LOCAL_EVALUATION_FINAL.md

# P3 keeps a second canonical log, for the same reason the first one is tracked: a measurement is
# only citable if the records are in the repository. It lives in its own directory rather than in the
# root log because the 42 records there are frozen historical evidence, and appending to them would
# invalidate a published SHA-256. The directory is re-included first, then everything inside it is
# ignored again, then the log alone is tracked -- a negation cannot resurrect a file whose parent
# directory is ignored, so the three lines have to come in this order.
!results/p3_boundary_locus/
results/p3_boundary_locus/*
!results/p3_boundary_locus/usage.jsonl
